



:: warrior_queen_day_4_1
<<set $warrior_queen_arc_step = 4>>
/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
DAY 4   ARCHERY REIMAGINED
Part 1: The Iron Garden   Morning Drill
═══════════════════════════════════════════════ */

The rapier bit the air where Aldric's throat had been. He was already gone   pivoted three inches to the right with the languid economy of a man who could dodge death between heartbeats and still have time to yawn   and his practice blade came sweeping laterally toward my exposed ribs.

I cross-stepped. Left foot over right, hips turning, the feminine guard carrying me out of the line as smoothly as a boat sliding from a dock. The blade passed through the space I had vacated and I riposted   the rapier snaking forward from the seconda guard, point reaching for the gap between his arm and torso.

He parried. Barely. The flat of his practice blade batted the rapier aside a half-second before the point would have touched his ribs.

<<speech "aldric">>Faster. Your second step is still dragging.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>My second step is //tired//. Every part of me is tired. My second step is considering mutiny.<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Tell your second step that mutiny is punishable by additional drills.<</speech>>

We had been at it since before dawn   an hour of drill that had begun with the feminine guard stance-hold and progressed into the three-guard sequence Aldric had introduced yesterday. Prima, seconda, terza. Transition between them smoothly, without resetting. Each guard flowing into the next like stanzas of a violent poem.

The training corset held me firm beneath the leathers. I had laced it myself this morning   still poorly, the ties slightly uneven, the left cup sitting a fraction higher than the right   but the improvement over Maera's binding was categorical. The breasts, which had spent yesterday bouncing during the rapier drills with enough enthusiasm to entertain the soldiers on the rampart, were compressed, cradled, and //silent//. No bouncing. No shifting. The small mounds of tender flesh sat in their padded cups like passengers in a carriage, aware of the journey but insulated from the road.

The nipples, however, had opinions about the morning chill.

They had hardened the instant I stepped into the Iron Garden's pre-dawn cold   puckering beneath the corset's padding into stiff peaks that pressed outward against the lambskin lining with a persistence that was becoming deeply familiar. The padding muted the sensation   thank every god for Veletia's attention to that particular detail   but I could still //feel// them. Two points of heightened awareness on a chest that was becoming, day by day, a landscape of increasingly complex sensation.

I lunged. Prima guard   blade high, off-hand raised behind me like a dancer's counterweight. The thrust was clean, driven from the hips, the new centre of gravity pulling the motion downward and //forward// in a way that the old, shoulder-driven lunge never had. The rapier's point stopped an inch from Aldric's breastplate.

He looked down at it.

<<speech "aldric">>Adequate.<</speech>>

From Aldric, //adequate// was a standing ovation.

[[Continue the morning drill|warrior_queen_day_4_2]]

:: warrior_queen_day_4_2

We drilled the three-guard transition for another twenty minutes. I was drenched   sweat running in rivers down my spine, soaking the linen beneath the corset, darkening the leather of the tunic. The corset itself was damp, the lambskin lining warm against breast skin that was flushed with exertion. Each breath pulled the boning tight across my ribs and then released it, a rhythmic compression-and-expansion that the growing tissue had started to... accommodate. Not enjoy, precisely. But accept. The way one accepts the rhythm of a heartbeat   present, persistent, eventually invisible.

The sand sack was absent today. Aldric had decided, after yesterday's session, that the corset alone provided sufficient simulation for the current stage of breast development. The sand sack would return, he warned, as the breasts grew   as the weight increased beyond what the natural tissue currently provided. For now, my own small swells, compressed and cradled in the cups, were enough.

//Enough.// As though having breasts at all   having flesh on my chest that bounced and ached and hardened in the cold and responded to touch with a sensitivity that still startled me every time I applied the salve   was a matter of //sufficient weight// rather than a complete overhaul of every assumption I had ever held about the body I lived in.

<<speech "aldric">>Hold.<</speech>>

I froze mid-transition, rapier halfway between seconda and terza. The sweat dripped from my chin. The braid had loosened   strands of hair falling across my face, catching on my eyelashes. I blew them aside with a puff of breath that came out higher-pitched than intended.

Aldric sheathed his practice blade and turned toward the yard entrance.

<<speech "aldric">>There is someone I wish you to meet.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Who?<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Someone who understands a problem you do not yet know you have.<</speech>>

He walked toward the gate. I stood in the Iron Garden's centre, rapier in hand, sweat cooling on skin that was softer than it should have been, and watched him go. The sun was climbing the eastern wall. The mist was burning off. Somewhere in the castle, a bell tolled the morning hour.

I had approximately thirty seconds of peace before the most terrifying woman I had ever met walked into the training yard.

[[Dame Yseldra arrives|warrior_queen_day_4_3]]

:: warrior_queen_day_4_3

Dame Yseldra Thornehart entered the Iron Garden as though she had been born from its cobblestones   fully formed, fully armoured, and fully unimpressed with everything she surveyed.

She was tall. Taller than Aldric, which was itself a feat requiring either exceptional genetics or a pact with gods of unusual generosity. Her braided blonde hair was imprisoned beneath a steel circlet that caught the morning light and threw it back with interest. Her spear was absent, but a recurve bow rode in her left hand with the easy lethality that other women reserved for a fan or a flower. She wore half-plate over boiled leather, and every piece of it was immaculate   oiled, maintained, and arranged with the precision of a woman who considered imperfection a personal insult.

She regarded me with ice-blue eyes that weighed, measured, and found wanting. All in the space of a single heartbeat.

<<speech "yseldra">>So. The prince who would be queen.<</speech>>

I straightened. The rapier was still in my hand. The sweat was still on my face. The breasts, beneath the corset and the leathers, were still //there//. I could not command their absence any more than I could command the tide.

<<speech "mc">>The heir. Who //shall// be queen.<</speech>>

<<speech "yseldra">>Bold words for someone whose braid is falling apart.<</speech>>

I reached back. She was right   the braid had loosened into a tangled suggestion of organisation, strands escaping in every direction. I tugged the leather tie free, gathered the mass   heavier, thicker, longer than it had been even a week ago   and retied it with fingers that fumbled.

Aldric reappeared beside Yseldra. He had materialised silently, as was his deeply unfair custom.

<<speech "aldric">>Dame Yseldra Thornehart. Inner Court Sentinel. The finest archer in Velmoria. Possibly the finest in the realm.<</speech>>

<<speech "yseldra">>Possibly.<</speech>> 

She said it without modesty or boast   a simple acknowledgment of probability, like a mathematician stating an axiom.

<<speech "aldric">>She will teach you the bow. I shall return at sundown.<</speech>>

He departed as though the matter were routine. Which, for Aldric, it likely was. The man delegated with the same efficiency he employed in ending lives   quickly, cleanly, and without discussion.

I was alone with Dame Yseldra Thornehart.

The temperature in the Iron Garden dropped approximately five degrees, which I attributed to her presence rather than any meteorological event.

[[Yseldra's assessment|warrior_queen_day_4_4]]

:: warrior_queen_day_4_4

She circled me. Slowly. With the same patient, predatory orbit that Aldric employed, but where Aldric circled like a wolf assessing prey, Yseldra circled like a hawk   high, detached, seeing everything from an altitude that rendered the subject small and entirely knowable.

<<speech "yseldra">>Turn.<</speech>>

I turned. Presented my back.

<<speech "yseldra">>Arms out.<</speech>>

I extended my arms. The posture stretched the training corset across my chest, the boning pulling slightly, the cups adjusting to the new angle. I could feel the breast tissue shift within the cups   a small lateral migration as the arms extended, drawing the pectoral muscle outward and carrying the attached tissue with it. A movement I would not have noticed a month ago. A movement I now catalogued automatically, filed under the growing library of //things the breasts do//.

<<speech "yseldra">>Hm.<</speech>>

She completed the circuit and stood before me. Her eyes   pale blue, the colour of winter ice at its thinnest   tracked from my shoulders to my hands to my chest to my hips with the detached precision of an archer measuring a target.

<<speech "yseldra">>Your shoulders have narrowed. Two inches, perhaps more, since last I saw you at the harvest tournament. The deltoids have thinned. The biceps are leaner. The forearms are  <</speech>> 

she seized my right forearm without permission, turned it, examined the underside 

<<speech "yseldra">>  still strong, but the wrist joint has loosened. More flexibility. Less rigidity. Good for the rapier. Problematic for the draw.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>The draw?<</speech>>

She released my arm and walked toward the weapons rack at the yard's edge. From it, she lifted a recurve bow   not the massive seventy-pound war bow I had trained with since seventeen, but something lighter. A hunting bow, perhaps, or a cavalry bow. The limbs were shorter, the grip narrower, the draw weight... lower.

<<speech "yseldra">>Fifty pounds,<</speech>> 

she said, answering the question I had not asked. 

<<speech "yseldra">>Your old bow was seventy. That draw weight was calibrated for broad shoulders, thick deltoids, and a flat chest that offered the string an unobstructed path from hand to anchor. You no longer possess any of those things.<</speech>>

The observation landed like a slap delivered with surgical precision.

<<speech "yseldra">>The shoulders cannot sustain a seventy-pound draw at full extension. Not anymore. Not with the muscle you have lost and the flexibility you have gained. We begin at fifty and rebuild from there. Take it.<</speech>>

[[Take the bow|warrior_queen_day_4_5]]

:: warrior_queen_day_4_5

I took the bow. It was lighter than my old one   significantly so. The grip was narrower, designed for a hand that was itself becoming narrower, the fingers wrapping around the leather with a fit that was... better than the old bow had provided in weeks. My palm had thinned. The old grip had been sitting in a hand that was slowly outgrowing it   or rather, //ingrowing// it, the hand shrinking while the grip remained constant.

This grip //fit//. My fingers found their positions naturally. The leather seated against a palm that was smoother, softer, but no less capable of holding tension.

<<speech "yseldra">>Nock an arrow.<</speech>>

She produced a quiver from the weapons rack and handed it to me. I slung it over my shoulder   the strap crossing my chest diagonally, running between my breasts, pressing the corset flat against the left cup   and pulled an arrow.

I nocked it. Set my feet in the old wide stance, the one drilled into me since the first day I picked up a bow at thirteen. I drew.

The string came back. Fifty pounds was lighter   noticeably, almost embarrassingly so. Where seventy required the old shoulders to //work//, to brace and lock and engage every fibre of deltoid and trapezius, fifty came smoothly. The muscles engaged but did not strain. The draw felt... manageable.

I anchored. Two fingers at the corner of my jaw. String brushing the chin. Arm extended in a clean line from elbow through shaft to target.

The form was perfect. Textbook. The form that had won me three harvest tournament ribbons.

The string crossed my chest.

[[The bowstring|warrior_queen_day_4_6]]

:: warrior_queen_day_4_6

Pain //detonated// through my left breast like a heated brand pressed to the skin.

The bowstring, on its path from hand to jaw, had dragged directly across the swell of the left breast   not the flat, ignorable chest of the prince I had been, but flesh that was budding and nerve-rich and exquisitely, //unforgivingly// tender, even through the training corset's padding. The string had caught the outermost curve of the cup, compressed it inward, and scraped across the tissue beneath with a friction that translated through lambskin and linen into pure, crystalline agony.

I yelped.

The sound that left my throat was   I will deny it to my dying breath, but the truth must be recorded somewhere   a //squeak//. Not a grunt, not a bark, not the masculine exclamation of pain that the prince would have produced. A squeak. High, sharp, involuntary, and pitched in a register that the prince's vocal cords had never visited and that my current vocal cords were visiting with increasing frequency.

The arrow loosed wild. It sailed high over the target   over the //yard//   and buried itself in a wooden fence post thirty yards beyond with a distant, mocking //thunk// that echoed off the castle walls like laughter.

I lowered the bow. Breathed through my teeth. Breathed //hard//, because the left breast was on fire. Not the tender ache of growth, not the compression-throb of the corset, but a sharp, electric, //specific// agony that radiated outward from the point of impact in pulsing waves.

Even through the corset   through the cups, through the padding, through the lambskin lining designed expressly to protect the tissue   the bowstring had found its target with a precision that would have been admirable if it were not agonising.

I pressed my hand against the left breast. Gently. Through the leather tunic. Through the corset. Through the layers of protection that had proven, in this singular case, entirely insufficient. The flesh beneath was //hot//. Swollen further in protest and the nipple   buried behind layers of fabric and padding   was sending a bright, continuous distress signal that ran from the tip of the breast through the ribcage and directly into the pain centres of my brain.

<<speech "mc">>Gods   nnhh   <</speech>>

The curse came out as a hiss. I clenched my jaw. The breast throbbed.

[[Yseldra's response|warrior_queen_day_4_7]]

:: warrior_queen_day_4_7

Yseldra did not laugh. She did not smirk. She did, however, allow herself a single dry exhalation through her nose that might have been a distant relative of amusement, visiting from distant provinces and unsure of the local customs.

<<speech "yseldra">>Every girl learns this lesson.<</speech>>

She said it flatly. Without sympathy. Without cruelty. With the precise neutrality of a woman stating a fact that was, to her, as evident as gravity.

<<speech "yseldra">>Most learn it at thirteen, when the buds first appear and they reach for their first bow without thinking. The string finds the breast. The breast objects. The girl learns. You are late to the lesson, but the lesson is the same.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>You might have //warned// me.<</speech>>

<<speech "yseldra">>I could have described the sensation in exacting detail and you would have nodded and drawn exactly as you did, because men   and those who were recently men   do not believe warnings about breast pain until the breast produces pain. It is a universal constant. The warning would have wasted both our time. The bruise will not.<</speech>>

She was, infuriatingly, correct.

<<speech "yseldra">>Lower the bow. Face me.<</speech>>

I lowered the bow. Faced her. The left breast was throbbing in rhythm with my heartbeat   a deep, pulsing ache that I could feel radiating outward from the impact site, through the cup, through the corset, through the layers of my awareness. The welt from the string would be forming now, beneath all that protection. A thin line of angry red across the curve of new flesh. A line that would ache for days.

Yseldra stepped behind me. Without warning or preamble, she seized my draw arm   the right   and //wrenched// it upward.

<<speech "mc">>Ow  <</speech>>

<<speech "yseldra">>The elbow. Higher. Here.<</speech>>

She physically repositioned the arm. The adjustment was precise, unyielding, and entirely without sympathy. She angled the forearm so that the bowstring's path would arc //above// the breast line rather than across it. The elbow rose to the height of my ear. The draw arm twisted into a position that felt like someone had rearranged my skeleton while I wasn't looking.

<<speech "yseldra">>Your anchor point is wrong. The jaw corner was correct for a flat chest. You no longer have a flat chest. The new anchor is here  <</speech>>

She pressed her fingertip against my cheekbone. Hard. The contact was a statement, not a caress.

<<speech "yseldra">>  the cheekbone. Higher anchor means higher string path. Higher string path means the string passes above the breast instead of across it. You will lose your old sight picture. You will lose your old aiming instincts. Everything you know about shooting is now wrong. Welcome to archery with breasts.<</speech>>

[[Learn the feminine draw|warrior_queen_day_4_8]]

:: warrior_queen_day_4_8

The feminine draw felt like trying to write with my feet whilst standing on my head in a stiff wind.

Everything was //wrong//. The elbow position   high, tucked close to the skull   strained muscles in the shoulder that the old draw had never troubled. The shoulder itself was narrower than it had been, the deltoid thinner, and the new position demanded engagement of the trapezius in a way that immediately began to burn with a deep, structural ache.

The cheekbone anchor was unstable. The jaw corner had provided a solid, bony shelf for the string hand   two fingers seated against bone, reliable, unchanging. The cheekbone was higher, shallower, and the angle of approach meant the string pressed against skin that was   I noticed with a start   //softer// than it had been. The cheekbone itself was higher now, more prominent, the facial structure shifting beneath Thalara's potions into something with less jaw and more cheek. The anchor that had been bone-against-finger was becoming bone-against-softer-finger-against-softer-skin and the sight picture. Gods, the sight picture.

My old aim had been calibrated over seven years and approximately ten thousand arrows. The jaw anchor and level elbow produced a specific line from eye through shaft to target that was burned into my nervous system like a brand. The cheekbone anchor moved everything   the angle of the shaft, the trajectory of the arrow, the relationship between eye and target. I was not merely learning a new anchor. I was //unlearning// an entire ballistic system and replacing it from scratch.

I drew. Aimed. Released.

The arrow hit the wall. Not the target, not the target frame, not the hay bales behind the target. The //wall//. Four feet above the target and two feet to the left, burying itself in the mortar between two stones with a //crack// that sent chips of masonry showering onto the cobblestones.

<<speech "yseldra">>Again.<</speech>>

I drew. The string hummed above my left breast   I could feel it hovering there, a hair's breadth of clearance between taut bowstring and tender flesh, the proximity generating a low thrum of anxiety in the tissue that had so recently been introduced to the string's displeasure.

I aimed. Released.

The arrow hit the hay bale. Not the target painted upon it, but the bale itself. A marginal improvement.

<<speech "yseldra">>Again.<</speech>>

[[Drilling the draw|warrior_queen_day_4_9]]

:: warrior_queen_day_4_9

We drilled for two hours.

Yseldra was   and I say this with the cautious respect one affords a natural disaster   //relentless//. She made Aldric look like a kindly uncle distributing sweets on feast days. Where Aldric corrected through laconic instruction, Yseldra corrected through physical adjustment   seizing my elbow, wrenching my shoulder, pressing my spine into alignment with a palm between the shoulder blades that brooked no argument.

Her hands were impersonal. Efficient. The hands of a warrior adjusting a weapon, not touching a body. But the body she was adjusting was a body in transition, and every point of contact reminded me of things I could not ignore.

When she pressed my shoulder back, the motion pulled the corset tight across my chest, and the breast tissue compressed against the cups with a sensation that was half-ache, half-warmth.

When she adjusted my stance   feet narrower, weight dropped into the hips, the feminine guard adapted for archery   her boot nudged my trailing foot, and I was aware of the way my hips //accepted// the narrower stance more readily than they accepted the old wide one. The body knew what it wanted to stand like. The mind was still arguing.

When she repositioned my draw arm for the fourteenth time, her fingers brushed the quiver strap that crossed my chest, and the strap shifted against the corset, and the corset shifted against the breasts, and I felt a chain reaction of tiny adjustments ripple through the tissue   each breast resettling in its cup, the flesh conforming to the new angle like water conforming to a tilted glass.

By the end of the second hour, I was hitting the target.

Not the centre. Not with any consistency. But the arrows were landing within the painted rings   third ring, fourth ring, the occasional glorious second-ring strike that made Yseldra's mouth twitch by approximately one millimetre.

My shoulder was screaming. The higher elbow position had engaged muscles that had spent twenty years in comfortable retirement, and they were not pleased about the conscription. The trapezius on the right side was locked in a cramp that sent roots of pain down through my shoulder blade and into the ribs. The deltoid   thinner, weaker, the muscle mass slowly dissolving under the potions' patient alchemy   trembled with each draw.

But the breast was safe. The string arced cleanly above the left cup, clearing the tissue by an inch   more clearance than strictly necessary, but I was //generous// with that inch. The memory of the impact was too fresh, the welt still throbbing beneath the padding, and I would rather miss every target in the yard than feel that brand-hot agony again.

<<speech "yseldra">>Halt.<</speech>>

I lowered the bow. My arms dropped to my sides with the limp gratitude of muscles that had been held at tension for too long.

Yseldra studied me. Her ice-blue eyes catalogued the sweat, the trembling, the loosened braid, the way I was unconsciously pressing my left arm against my chest to guard the injured breast.

<<speech "yseldra">>The draw improves. The aim is atrocious. But you are not hitting yourself, which is progress.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>High praise.<</speech>>

<<speech "yseldra">>The highest I intend to offer. Sit. Drink and when you have caught your breath, we will discuss equipment.<</speech>>

[[Water break and equipment|warrior_queen_day_4_10]]

:: warrior_queen_day_4_10

I sat on the weapons rack   the same rack Aldric perched upon, which had apparently become the designated location for instructors to deliver uncomfortable truths   and drank from the water jug. The water was cold and tasted like the stones of the cistern. I drank until my stomach sloshed.

Yseldra remained standing. She had not sat down once during the entire session. I was beginning to suspect the woman might not actually possess the ability to sit, as though the concept had been omitted from her construction.

<<speech "yseldra">>You need a breast guard.<</speech>>

The statement landed in the Iron Garden with the force of a practice blade to the ribs. I nearly choked on the water.

<<speech "mc">>I   what?<</speech>>

<<speech "yseldra">>A breast guard. Boiled leather, shaped to cup and compress. Worn beneath the tunic for every draw until the day you die or cease to shoot, whichever comes first.<</speech>>

She said it the way one might describe the necessity of stringing a bow before using it   as an obvious, mechanical requirement that only a fool would question.

<<speech "yseldra">>The corset provides adequate support for swordwork, but archery requires specific protection. The string's path, even with the feminine draw, will graze the breast on occasion   during high wind, during rapid shooting, during the hundred small misalignments that occur when you are tired or frightened or shooting at something that is trying to kill you. The breast guard eliminates the risk. It flattens and shields the tissue against the string's contact.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>I have only been... I mean, they are not large enough to require  <</speech>>

<<speech "yseldra">>They are large enough. If the string found them today through a training corset, they are large enough and they will be larger tomorrow, and the day after, and the month after that. The guard is not negotiable. I shall speak to the armourer myself.<</speech>>

She paused and then, with a precision that suggested the information was so obvious it barely warranted breath:

<<speech "yseldra">>There is no shame in the wearing. Every woman archer in every army in every kingdom upon this earth wears one. It is no different than a codpiece, save the location.<</speech>>

The comparison startled a laugh from me.

A real laugh. Not the bitter, sharp exhalation that had served as laughter these past weeks, but a genuine sound   warm and unburdened and //surprised//. It rose from the belly, exactly where Aldric had taught me to breathe, and emerged from a throat that shaped it differently than it had a month ago. Higher. Lighter. Breathier. Not a man's laugh and not quite a woman's, but something in between   something that was becoming its own thing, as all of me was becoming its own thing.

Yseldra's mouth twitched. Just barely. The barest ghost of warmth crossing a landscape that was otherwise Arctic tundra in expression. Then the ice returned and sealed her features shut.

<<speech "yseldra">>Do not mistake that for fondness. On the morrow I expect every arrow upon the target. Miss, and you shall run the battlements in full plate until you vomit. Now  <</speech>>

She nodded toward the archery range.

<<speech "yseldra">>Twenty more. The drill is the same and this time, I want the breath held at the top of the draw. Your chest is moving when you release   the expansion of the ribcage shifts the string path. Hold the breath. Still the chest. The breasts must not move during the release. If they move, the arrow moves. If the arrow moves, someone dies who should not have.<</speech>>

[[Twenty more arrows|warrior_queen_day_4_11]]

:: warrior_queen_day_4_11

Twenty more arrows. I shot them with my jaw clenched and my breath held at the top of each draw, the way Yseldra demanded.

She was right about the breathing. The chest-breath I had used my entire shooting life expanded the ribcage, which shifted the corset, which shifted the breast tissue, which shifted the string path by millimetres that translated to inches at thirty yards and feet at a hundred. The belly-breath Aldric had taught me   the same breath that anchored the feminine guard   kept the chest //still//. The ribcage held its shape. The breasts held their position within the cups. The string held its line.

Arrow eleven hit the second ring.

Arrow fifteen hit the first ring.

Arrow nineteen hit the centre.

I stared at the target. The arrow protruded from the painted bullseye   dead centre, the shaft still vibrating with the force of impact. The fletching was straight. The angle was true. A clean, undeniable strike.

From the cheekbone anchor. From the feminine draw. From a body with breasts and wider hips and a lower centre of gravity and a breath that lived in the belly instead of the chest.

<<speech "yseldra">>Acceptable.<</speech>>

From Yseldra, that word carried the same weight as Aldric's nod   currency hoarded and spent with a miser's calculation, worth more than any tournament ribbon I had ever won.

She walked to the target and withdrew the arrow. Examined its angle of entry with the critical attention of a jeweller examining a cut.

<<speech "yseldra">>Your old draw would have hit this target nine times in ten. Your new draw hits it once in twenty. But the once is clean. The form is there. The body has the potential. What it lacks is repetition.<</speech>>

She returned the arrow to my quiver. Her hand brushed my shoulder in the motion   accidental, perhaps, but the contact sparked awareness. Her armour was cold against my skin, even through the leather. The touch lasted less than a second. But I felt it radiate through the shoulder, down through the chest, as though the breast tissue were now a secondary nervous system running in parallel to the primary one, picking up signals the old body would have missed entirely.

<<speech "yseldra">>The armourer shall have the breast guard by tomorrow. Have it fitted before you come to the range. Tight. Snug. The leather should cup and compress   not crush, not flatten, but //hold//. You want the tissue immobilised during the draw, not punished for existing.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Not punished for existing. I shall try to remember that principle generally.<</speech>>

Yseldra looked at me. The ice-blue eyes thawed by precisely one degree.

<<speech "yseldra">>That principle applies to more than the breast guard, Your Highness. Remember it.<</speech>>

She turned and departed. Her armour did not clank. It //whispered//   oiled plate moving over oiled plate, each piece fitted to her body with the same relentless precision she brought to everything. She walked like a weapon that had been stored properly and was merely relocating to a different armoury.

I stood at the archery range alone, the bow hanging from my aching hand, and felt the left breast throb beneath the corset. The welt from the first draw was still there   still hot, still angry, still announcing itself with each heartbeat. I would need to examine it tonight. Apply the salve. Let Maera's careful fingers tend the damage.

The thought of Maera's fingers on the welt   on //that// spot, on the curve where the string had found the breast   sent a ripple of sensation through the tissue that I chose not to examine too closely.

[[Return to the corridor|warrior_queen_day_4_12]]

:: warrior_queen_day_4_12

The corridor from the Iron Garden to the main castle was cool and dim. Arrow-slit windows threw bars of light across the flagstones. My boots   slightly loose now, the feet having narrowed   echoed with each step. I passed the entrance to the Great Library and paused.

Through the heavy door, half-ajar, I could hear the scratch of a quill. A throat cleared. The rustle of parchment.

Jeral.

I pushed the door open. The library's vast interior stretched before me   shelves climbing three storeys, ladders and catwalks and the dusty, breathing hush of ten thousand books existing in quiet communion. Jeral sat at his customary desk near the window, surrounded by scrolls, his spectacles perched on the very tip of his thin nose in a position that defied both gravity and good sense. He looked up.

<<speech "jeral">>Your Highness. You appear to have been shot.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Nearly. By myself, in a manner of speaking.<</speech>>

I leaned against the doorframe. My arms ached. My shoulder burned. The breast throbbed. But there was comfort in Jeral's presence   the man was a pool of calm in a castle of storms. Nothing rattled him. Nothing surprised him. He existed in a state of perpetual, gentle expectation that everything would be exactly as complicated as it always was, and his only task was to record the complications accurately.

<<speech "jeral">>Dame Yseldra, I presume. I noted her arrival this morning. She is not a woman who enters a building quietly, despite all evidence suggesting she believes she does.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>She taught me the feminine draw. The archery form for... for someone with...<</speech>>

I gestured vaguely at my chest. Jeral's eyes did not flicker. Did not drop. Did not acknowledge the gesture with anything other than an infinitesimal adjustment of his spectacles.

<<speech "jeral">>For someone whose anatomy has changed in ways that affect the string's path. Yes. I have read about the feminine draw in Lady Orenthia's //Treatise on the Queen's Blades//. Third floor, east alcove, second shelf from the top. Published three hundred years ago and largely ignored since, which is the fate of most texts written by women about women's combat techniques. Dame Yseldra is one of perhaps four people in the kingdom who still practice it.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>You knew she was coming.<</speech>>

<<speech "jeral">>I knew Sir Aldric had requested her presence through the duty roster, which I maintain. I drew the obvious inference. It is not difficult when one pays attention, which most people decline to do on principle.<</speech>>

